<h4 class="western">In chapter but not in this extract</h4>
<h2 class="chapterheading-western">&nbsp;</h2>
<ul>
<li class="western">Naive and Aware Dates and Times</li>
<li class="western">Datetime Module</li>
<li class="western">Timestamps</li>
<li class="western">Today and Now</li>
</ul>
<h2 class="western">Time <strong>Z</strong>ones and Aware Datetime Objects</h2>
<p class="western">So far we have ignored the problem of timezones. All datetime objects have been naive, simply recordings of date and time data without any reference as to where they are valid. This often means that they are treated by external programs as local dates and times. This is fine as long as you are happy working with the system’s default timezone, but it is better to take control of this variability.</p>
<p class="western">The datetime and time objects have a tzinfo attribute which is set to None in naive objects. You can set tzinfo to any of the standard timezones, but this is slightly more complicated than you might expect. The datetime module only provides an abstract class for you to implement your own timezone objects and a single implementation of the class as timezone. In most cases you won’t want to implement your own timezone definitions and using the timezone object is a better option’ but it is too simple for some applications as it doesn’t allow daylight saving to be specified.<br clear="left" /> Another reason is that time zones are far more complicated than you might expect:</p>
<p class="western"><img src="/images/stories/Core/Pythonlang/timezones.jpg" alt="timezones" width="400" height="216" /></p>
<p class="western">In most cases it is better to use the zoneinfo module which implements the full IANA time zone database. This was introduced in Python 3.9 and it is to be preferred to the older tzdata module. IANA, the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority, has created a language-independent database of time zones including information on daylight saving. If a government changes its time zone or its daylight saving hours and dates this is communicated to IANA and the database is updated. While zoneinfo can make use of the older tzdata module for its database, you should install the IANA database. While some operating systems have the database installed as standard others, notably Windows, do not. The database has to be installed and Python has to be able to find it with the help of some additional path handling.</p>
<p class="western">The easiest way to get the database installed is to use:</p>
<pre class="western">pip install tzdata</pre>
<p class="western">As long as you have only one version of Python installed, this will copy the database into its lib\site-packages directory. If you have multiple Python installations you can use:</p>
<pre class="western">py -3.10-64  -m pip install tzdata</pre>
<p class="western">where you replace the -3.10-64 with the Python version you are using. You can check where pip has installed the database using:</p>
<pre class="western">pip show tzdata</pre>
<p class="western">If Python cannot find the database you’ll need to update the path information, see the documentation.</p>
<p class="western">You can find out what time zones are in the database using:</p>
<pre class="western">tz=zoneinfo.available_timezones()
print(tz)</pre>
<p class="western">or you can view the full current list at the IANA website.</p>
<p class="western">You can specify the time zone for any datetime or time object using the tzinfo parameter. For example:</p>
<pre class="western">import datetime
import zoneinfo
dt = datetime.datetime(2022, 1, 14, 10,50,
                    tzinfo = zoneinfo.ZoneInfo("Europe/Madrid"))
print(dt)</pre>
<p class="western">displays:</p>
<pre class="western">2022-01-14 10:50:00+01:00</pre>
<p class="western">and the +01:00 indicates that Spain is one hour ahead of UTC.</p>
<p class="western">Once you define a datetime object with tzinfo set to an instance that sets the tzinfo.utcoffset to an integer it is treated as a timezone-aware object. This doesn’t change its meaning very much – the datetime object records the date and time in that time zone. It is only when you try to mix dates and times from different time zones do things behave differently. In particular conversion to UTC is performed taking the time zone into account.</p>
<p class="western">For example;</p>
<pre class="western">import datetime
import zoneinfo
dt1 = datetime.datetime(2022, 1, 14, 10,50, <br />
tzinfo = zoneinfo.ZoneInfo("Europe/Madrid"))
dt2 = datetime.datetime(2022, 1, 14, 10,50, 
                     tzinfo = zoneinfo.ZoneInfo("utc"))
print(dt1.timestamp())
print(dt2.timestamp())</pre>
<p class="western">displays</p>
<pre class="western">1642153800.0
1642157400.0</pre>
<p class="western">which differ by exactly 3600 seconds, i.e one hour.</p>
<p class="western">Notice that you can specify the time zone to be used, rather than the local time zone, in many methods. For example:</p>
<pre class="western">fromtimestamp(timestamp,tz = <em>timezone</em>)</pre>
<p class="western">will convert the timestamp which is UTC, to whatever <em>timezone</em> you specify. It is good practice to explicitly use a “utc” time zone, even if your local time is UTC.</p>
